WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.013 --> 00:00:18.322
"It's a perfect day to walk across the Brooklyn Bridge," Paul said.

2
00:00:18.323 --> 00:00:26.009
He looked up at the enormous bridge that connected two very busy parts of New York City—Brooklyn and Manhattan.

3
00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.916
The bridge was pretty old now.

4
00:00:27.917 --> 00:00:36.087
But with its tall stone towers and strong-looking cables, it was still an amazing sight above the East River.

5
00:00:36.088 --> 00:00:40.636
"Dad, who built this bridge?" Paul asked.

6
00:00:40.637 --> 00:00:44.905
"A man named John Roebling," his father answered.

7
00:00:44.906 --> 00:00:49.615
"Now Brooklyn and Manhattan are part of the same city—New York.

8
00:00:49.616 --> 00:00:52.558
But they used to be two different cities.

9
00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:59.782
In the 1800s boats would bring people back and forth across the East River to work, shop, or visit friends.

10
00:00:59.783 --> 00:01:03.735
The river was sometimes rough, and the boats were often late.

11
00:01:03.736 --> 00:01:11.088
One day John was on one of those boats with his son, and he got the idea to build a suspension bridge across the river.

12
00:01:11.089 --> 00:01:14.233
Many people said it couldn't be done.

13
00:01:14.234 --> 00:01:18.779
But one winter the East River froze, and the boats couldn't get through.

14
00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:22.281
People from Brooklyn couldn't get to work in Manhattan.

15
00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:27.805
Finally it was decided that a bridge should be built, connecting the two places."

16
00:01:27.806 --> 00:01:32.929
"Was John Roebling an engineer like you?" asked Paul.

17
00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:37.292
"Yes, he was. His son, Washington, was an engineer too.

18
00:01:37.293 --> 00:01:41.076
In 1869 they started to work on the bridge together.

19
00:01:41.077 --> 00:01:44.602
But sadly, a freak accident happened.

20
00:01:44.603 --> 00:01:50.169
While John Roebling was on the Brooklyn side of the river, his foot got caught in the dock.

21
00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:54.087
An incoming boat crashed into the dock, injuring him badly.

22
00:01:54.088 --> 00:01:57.881
He died a month later. So Washington took over."

23
00:01:57.882 --> 00:02:03.839
"Washington must have been sad when his father died," murmured Paul.

24
00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.771
"Yes, he and his father were very close.

25
00:02:06.772 --> 00:02:09.688
But Washington was ready for the job.

26
00:02:09.689 --> 00:02:15.865
He started working right away, digging deep under the river mud to create supports for the two bridge towers.

27
00:02:15.866 --> 00:02:18.503
This almost cost him his life."

28
00:02:18.504 --> 00:02:24.344
Paul gasped. "You mean, Washington almost died too?"

29
00:02:24.345 --> 00:02:29.835
"Yes, he designed something called a caisson to use for the underwater project.

30
00:02:29.836 --> 00:02:36.135
These caissons, which prevented the water from flooding the work area, were filled with compressed air.

31
00:02:36.136 --> 00:02:43.997
And at the time, people didn't realize that working in compressed air can cause caisson disease, or decompression sickness.

32
00:02:43.998 --> 00:02:49.089
That's a dangerous condition that results from gas bubbles forming inside the body.

33
00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:55.818
Caisson disease affected Washington's nervous system, paralyzing him for a time and causing him great pain.

34
00:02:55.819 --> 00:02:59.693
Some members of his work crew even died from the illness!

35
00:02:59.694 --> 00:03:05.093
Washington never returned to the bridge, but he continued to manage the project.

36
00:03:05.094 --> 00:03:11.473
Imagine this—he used a telescope to watch the progress of the bridge construction from his home in Brooklyn!"

37
00:03:11.474 --> 00:03:19.706
"Wow. It sounds like he was pretty sick—how could he manage everything from home?" Paul asked.

38
00:03:19.707 --> 00:03:25.570
"Good question," his father said. "Washington's wife, Emily, helped him.

39
00:03:25.571 --> 00:03:28.890
She wrote his letters and helped with planning the work.

40
00:03:28.891 --> 00:03:33.917
She served as his eyes and ears, and delivered all his orders to the workers.

41
00:03:33.918 --> 00:03:38.510
She even studied complicated mathematics to learn more about building!

42
00:03:38.511 --> 00:03:42.592
Soon she earned the respect of everyone involved in the project.

43
00:03:42.593 --> 00:03:47.111
If it weren't for her, the Brooklyn Bridge probably wouldn't have been finished."

44
00:03:47.112 --> 00:03:50.324
Paul's father pointed to the bridge towers.

45
00:03:50.325 --> 00:03:56.478
"When those towers were finished, they were 25 stories high, taller than any of the other buildings around them.

46
00:03:56.479 --> 00:03:58.665
Can you imagine that?"

47
00:03:58.666 --> 00:04:01.459
Paul looked at the city skyline.

48
00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:06.330
All the huge skyscrapers made the bridge towers look small now.

49
00:04:06.331 --> 00:04:10.688
He tried to imagine how different the city must have looked back then.

50
00:04:10.689 --> 00:04:17.554
"See those four main cables that run along the top from one tower to the other?" his father went on.

51
00:04:17.555 --> 00:04:20.456
"Can you spot all the wires hanging down from them?

52
00:04:20.457 --> 00:04:25.193
John Roebling invented their material, which is called wire rope.

53
00:04:25.194 --> 00:04:30.966
The wire rope is made of steel, so it is strong and can easily be bent into any shape.

54
00:04:30.967 --> 00:04:34.613
The Brooklyn Bridge was the first bridge where it was used.

55
00:04:34.614 --> 00:04:38.232
But those slanted supports were Washington's idea.

56
00:04:38.233 --> 00:04:41.272
He added them to make sure the bridge was safe."

57
00:04:41.273 --> 00:04:45.834
"How long did it take to build the bridge?" asked Paul.

58
00:04:45.835 --> 00:04:51.993
"Fourteen years," answered his father. "It was an amazing undertaking.

59
00:04:51.994 --> 00:04:56.733
When it was finished, it was the longest suspension bridge in the world.

60
00:04:56.734 --> 00:05:04.858
At the opening day ceremony in 1883, Emily Roebling was given the honor of being the first person to cross the bridge.

61
00:05:04.859 --> 00:05:09.503
She rode in a horse and carriage from the Brooklyn side to the Manhattan side.

62
00:05:09.504 --> 00:05:12.918
The workers all cheered as she went by."

63
00:05:12.919 --> 00:05:17.423
"Her husband must have been proud," said Paul.

64
00:05:17.424 --> 00:05:19.661
"Yes, it was his idea.

65
00:05:19.662 --> 00:05:25.911
Crowds lined the streets of Manhattan and Brooklyn, and hundreds of ships were packed into the East River.

66
00:05:25.912 --> 00:05:32.831
On the Manhattan side, the president of the United States waited with the governor and the mayor of New York City.

67
00:05:32.832 --> 00:05:37.389
Then they met Emily and they all walked across the bridge together.

68
00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:42.494
After the ceremony they went to the Roebling house to honor Washington and his father.

69
00:05:42.495 --> 00:05:45.799
There was a grand show of fireworks that night.

70
00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:50.611
It was the greatest celebration ever held in America up until that time."

71
00:05:50.612 --> 00:05:54.265
"That sounds pretty cool," said Paul.

72
00:05:54.266 --> 00:05:56.485
His father agreed with a nod.

73
00:05:56.486 --> 00:06:01.843
"I'd like to help build a bridge like that one day," said Paul.

74
00:06:01.844 --> 00:06:04.157
His father beamed.

75
00:06:04.158 --> 00:06:11.616
"The Brooklyn Bridge inspired the building of many other bridges, allowing people to travel easily to many new places.

76
00:06:11.617 --> 00:06:16.546
It was seen as the greatest engineering achievement of the nineteenth century."

77
00:06:16.547 --> 00:06:22.243
"My teacher said the Brooklyn Bridge is a true American landmark."

78
00:06:22.244 --> 00:06:24.299
Paul grabbed his father's arm.

79
00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:34.091
"Come on, Dad! Let's see how fast we can get to the other side!"
